Skips the check of the byte order of the IP header's length when
mtr-packet sends its IPv4 probes over an ICMP datagram socket, as it
does on Radix, which has no raw sockets: the kernel writes that
socket's IP header, so the order is not mtr's to find. The check pings
127.0.0.1 from the socket that sends every probe, and binds the socket
first to the address getsockname() gives a UDP socket connected to
127.0.0.1, which is 127.0.0.1, as on Linux. Each probe after it then
went from a socket bound to the loopback address, which the routers'
errors about them did not reach: mtr named no router. Linux skips the
check altogether.

--- a/packet/probe_unix.c
+++ b/packet/probe_unix.c
@@ -149,6 +149,13 @@
     return;
 #endif
 
+    /*  The kernel writes a datagram socket's IP header, so there is no
+     *  byte order of its length to find; and the ping to localhost
+     *  would bind the socket that sends each probe to 127.0.0.1. */
+    if (!net_state->platform.ip4_socket_raw) {
+        return;
+    }
+
     memset(&param, 0, sizeof(struct probe_param_t));
     param.ip_version = 4;
     param.protocol = IPPROTO_ICMP;
